# Review: SpacePlugin

Most of the space lifecycle, object, schema and collection-path machinery exists, and the operations
the agent surface uses are well tested: `AddObject`, `AddType`, `AddRelation`, `RemoveObjects`,
`RemoveAllObjects` and `Create` all have unit tests. Collection paths and the type-section path are
tested in `util/`. The spec has drifted from the code in several places. `AddSchema` is now `AddType`,
and `Reset` is `RemoveAllObjects`. That operation keeps the properties and root collection and
garbage-collects the rest; it does not create a new epoch or truncate feeds. `AddObject` no longer
navigates or takes `hidden`/`targetNodeId`. `Open`/`Close` open and close the SDK space; they do not
navigate. `Join` opens the join dialog instead of redeeming the code.

Several items have no implementation. `DuplicateObject` fails with "not yet implemented", and
`CollectionOperation.Create` is declared with no handler (collections are created through a
`CreateObjectEntry` and `AddObject`). Nothing runs migrations on a `SetupMigration` event. Presence
state (`viewersByObject` / `viewersByIdentity`) is initialized and read by `SpacePresence`, but nothing
in the plugin ever writes to it. `CollectionArticle` sets `draggable={false}`, so there is no reorder.
`CollectionSection` and `RecordArticle` are simpler than the spec's components. No automated test runs
join, export/import, or the view field delete/restore round trip.

## Extensions

| Term      | URI                        |
| --------- | -------------------------- |
| `verdict` | `org.dxos.mdl.verdict@1.0` |

